[00:36.10]Conversation One
[00:38.61]M: Hi, I heard you're planning a project meeting but can't decide between online and face-to-face. 
[00:45.14]Need some input£¿
[00:46.66]W: Yes, exactly. 
[00:48.75]I've been going back and forth for days. 
[00:51.67]Both formats seem to have clear advantages, but I'm not sure which works better for our team's specific needs.
[01:00.06]M: Let's break it down. 
[01:01.87]Online meetings are definitely more convenient. 
[01:05.07]Everyone can join from wherever they are without commuting, which saves time and money, especially for those who work remotely.
[01:12.88]W: That's true. 
[01:14.23]And online platforms let us record the session, so anyone who misses it can watch later. 
[01:21.61]That's really helpful for documentation and for new team members who join later.
[01:27.67]M: Right. Plus, online meetings make it easier to include remote colleagues or even people in different time zones. 
[01:35.71]Accessibility is a big plus that face-to-face meetings simply can't offer.
[01:41.22]W: But I feel like online meetings lack personal connection. 
[01:45.17]You can't read body language or facial expressions as well, which matters for building trust and ensuring everyone is on the same page.
[01:54.95]M: I agree.
[01:56.30]Face-to-face meetings build stronger relationships. 
[01:59.22]When you're in the same room, people tend to stay more engaged and less distracted by emails, phones, or other digital notifications.
[02:08.36]W: Exactly. 
[02:09.78]And for creative brainstorming or important decisions, being together in person creates energy that's hard to replicate online. 
[02:18.62]The dynamic discussions just flow better.
[02:22.33]M: So maybe it depends on the purpose. 
[02:24.95]Routine check-ins and brief updates can be online, but critical strategy sessions or client meetings might deserve an in-person format.
[02:33.53]W: That makes sense. 
[02:35.41]For this project, we need to build trust with new members and make some key decisions about budget and timeline. 
[02:43.20]So maybe we should start with a face-to-face meeting.
[02:46.90]M: Good call. 
[02:48.38]And we can always follow up with shorter online check-ins later. 
[02:52.74]A mixed approach might give us the best of both worlds without burning everyone out.
[02:57.81]W: Agreed. 
[02:59.07]Thanks for helping me think this through. 
[03:01.53]I feel much more confident now.
[03:04.94]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[03:10.47]1. What is the woman's problem£¿
[03:27.04]2. What does the man say is a big advantage that online meetings have over face-to-face meetings£¿
[03:47.27]3. Why can face-to-face meetings create stronger relationships£¿
[04:06.76]4. What is a purpose of their project meeting according to the woman£¿
[04:25.53]Conversation Two
[04:27.75]M: Have you started working on Professor Nason's highway design assignment£¿
[04:31.91]I'm a bit overwhelmed by all the factors we need to cover. 
[04:35.41]There's just so much information to sort through.
[04:38.41]W: I've done some reading. 
[04:40.45]Basically, safe highway design involves shape, traffic management, and safety features. 
[04:46.92]We should organize our paper around those three main areas to keep it clear.
[04:52.64]M: That makes sense. 
[04:54.28]For road shape design, I think horizontal and vertical curves are really important. 
[04:59.51]A sharp curve or steep slope can increase accident risk, especially at high speeds when drivers have less time to react.
[05:06.94]W: Right. 
[05:08.12]And we can't forget sight distance. 
[05:10.95]Drivers need enough stopping distance to react to hazards of debris on the road. 
[05:16.35]If sight distance is inadequate, rear-end collisions become much more likely.
[05:22.35]M: Good point. 
[05:23.66]What about road surface and drainage£¿ 
[05:26.09]A wet or poorly drained road can make tires lose contact with the road. 
[05:30.49]That's extremely dangerous.
[05:32.55]W: Exactly. 
[05:34.05]Those are part of road shape design too. 
[05:37.24]Now for traffic management, strategies like dynamic pricing help reduce congestion during peak hours.
[05:44.78]M: And intersections are high-risk areas. 
[05:48.23]Using traffic circles instead of traditional intersections, plus dedicated turn lanes and clear signals, can significantly improve safety for all users.
[05:58.48]W: Don't forget safety features like crash barriers and guardrails. 
[06:03.24]They don't prevent accidents from happening, but they reduce injury severity when crashes do occur, which is still valuable.
[06:11.71]M: So our structure could be: first road shape design with curves, sight distance, and drainage; then traffic management strategies; finally road safety features. 
[06:23.08]That flows logically from basic design to active management to passive protection.
[06:28.39]W: Agreed. 
[06:29.48]And we should mention that user behavior matters too. 
[06:33.49]Drivers can be unpredictable, so designs must account for human error, like distracted driving or misjudging speeds.
[06:41.98]M: Let's split the sections. 
[06:44.03]You take road shape design, I'll handle traffic management, and we can write the safety features part together over the weekend.
[06:52.85]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
