[08:46.29]Passage One
[08:48.33]A group of food safety and health supporters issued a petition seeking to have the Food and Drug Administration, or FDA for short, ban Red No. 3 in food. 
[09:01.15]The earlier ban was ordered in 1990, after two studies showed Red No. 3 caused cancer in male rats eating high levels of the chemical. 
[09:11.37]At the time, however, the FDA only acted to ban the coloring mixture in products put on the skin or in drugs taken by mouth. 
[09:21.07]Health activists had long sought FDA approval to expand the ban to food products. 
[09:27.62]In November, nearly two dozen members of Congress sent a letter demanding that FDA officials ban Red No. 3. 
[09:36.70]Some of the most common products containing Red No. 3 include sweets, strawberry milk, strawberry protein drinks, etc. 
[09:46.10]The FDA said food manufacturers will have to remove the dye from their products until the next January.
[09:53.72]Other countries still permit certain uses of the dye. 
[09:57.75]But any imported products will still have to meet the new US requirement. 
[10:03.20]The International Association of Color Manufacturers has repeatedly said Red No. 3 is safe in levels usually taken in by humans. 
[10:13.36]The group pointed to research carried out by scientific experts from the United Nations and the World Health Organization. 
[10:21.94]One study, released in 2018, confirmed the safety of Red No. 3 in food. 
[10:28.77]Some food manufacturers have already changed their products to remove Red No. 3. 
[10:35.37]One example is Sensient Food Colors, a St. Louis-based supplier of food colors and flavorings. 
[10:42.67]In its place, the company uses beet juice, a dye made from insects and other coloring mixtures.
[10:51.73]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
