[15:28.65]Recording One
[15:31.20]Children's vocabulary is shrinking as reading loses out to screen time, according to Susie Dent, who is urging families to read, talk and play word games to boost language development. 
[15:44.73]The warning comes as the government prepares to issue its first advice to parents on how to manage screen use in under-fives, amid concerns that excessive screen time is damaging children's language development.
[15:59.24]¡°So many children are now falling behind,¡±Dent said.
[16:03.16]¡°The vocabulary gap is getting bigger and there is a real perception that vocabulary development is suffering and that impacts on learning.¡±
[16:12.93]Citing a 2023 Oxford University Press report that found that two in five pupils had fallen behind in vocabulary development, she said,¡°There is a huge perception that screen time is having a negative impact on vocabulary, and I think that's because it is taking away from reading time. The digital lives of our children are taking a greater and greater role and reading certainly is in decline.¡±
[16:42.54]¡°I'm not saying no to screens altogether,¡±Dent said.
[16:46.19]¡°I think technology has a huge role to play in enhancing children's vocabulary, if it's used in the right way. But I think reading is really suffering. For me the biggest worry is the widening gap between children who do read and who do have good vocabulary acquisition and those who don't. It's quite a well-known effect that children who read will then accelerate their vocabulary acquisition much more quickly and it's at a much higher rate.¡±
[17:17.60]It might be a crisis, but Dent said,¡°I would just say that there are ways that we can address it. Dialogue or conversation integrated into daily life is really important, also word-based games. This is another way in which actually going online can help, because there are so many brilliant word games out there.¡±
[17:41.16]Dent also believes that learning a foreign language can play a critical role in developing a love and understanding of English.
[17:49.91]¡°If there's one thing I could change, it would be to really promote the learning of foreign languages at school, because it's huge. The advantage it can give you is immeasurable.¡±
[18:02.75]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
