[19:05.80]Recording Two
[19:07.42]Remember the last time you really lost yourself in a book£¿ 
[19:11.11]Not skimming a page while scrolling your phone, but genuinely sinking into a story, a character's voice, or a new idea£¿ 
[19:18.92]For a lot of us, that feels like a long-lost luxury.
[19:22.78]But here's some good news: it doesn't have to stay that way. 
[19:26.37]In fact, with just a few shifts in mindset and a sprinkle of playful curiosity, you can rediscover the joy of reading¡ªand even make it fun again.
[19:36.17]First, let's remind ourselves why books are worth our time. 
[19:40.39]Leisure reading isn't just a hobby for¡°bookish¡±people. 
[19:43.82]It gives your brain a workout, helps you see the world through different lenses, and even lowers stress. 
[19:50.19]People who read fiction, for example, often find their ability to empathize with others gets a little boost. 
[19:57.06]And adult life can get pretty serious, so a bit of empathy and imagination can go a long way.
[20:04.44]Books engage us in ways that social media and short videos just can't. 
[20:09.33]They invite you to pause, immerse, reflect, and laugh...sometimes all in one chapter. 
[20:16.00]Sure, articles and TikToks are entertaining, but they rarely transport you somewhere new in the way a great story can.
[20:24.15]You're not weird if you've fallen out of the reading habit. 
[20:27.16]Lots of people do, especially during big life changes like starting a job, having kids, or moving house. 
[20:34.31]Sometimes the problem isn't a lack of time; it's that we try to read books that don't actually attract us. 
[20:40.91]Remember: reading for fun should feel fun. 
[20:44.32]If a book isn't sparking joy, enthusiasm, or curiosity, it's perfectly okay to put it down and try something else. 
[20:52.29]Trying to dive straight into a 700-page classic after months without leisure reading is like going from zero to marathon in a day. 
[21:00.44]Maybe instead, try shorter books or collections of short stories, satisfying reads that give you a sense of progress and completion without feeling overwhelmed. 
[21:10.46]You can try reading in five-minute bursts. 
[21:13.19]Set a timer and just read for five minutes, anywhere, anytime. 
[21:17.94]Before you know it, your brain will start associating reading with pleasure rather than effort.
[21:25.31]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[21:30.59]19. What is the talk mainly about£¿
[21:48.34]20. What is sometimes the real reason why people fall out of the reading habit£¿
[22:08.48]21. How should people restart reading£¿
[22:26.12]Recording Three
[22:28.66]Symptoms of aging can be shocking for children not expecting their parents to lose their abilities.
[22:35.31] What's important is learning ways to take care of the elderly people for their long, healthy, and happy lives. 
[22:42.92]Here, we are going to mention several essential needs of elderly people, including health, safety, emotional and social well-being and finances. 
[22:54.52]To satisfy all the needs, first things first, we should ensure proper nutrition and encourage regular exercise. 
[23:03.37]Elderly people often experience a loss of appetite, or several food restrictions due to medical conditions. 
[23:11.55]Consult a doctor or dietitian in such cases and then follow their advice. 
[23:17.73]Physical activity is important for all ages but especially for the elderly, as growing challenges with mobility, strength, and balance come with age. 
[23:29.26]However, not every exercise is recommended for them. 
[23:33.84]Simple exercises or moderate-intensity exercises like walking or chair exercises, stretching, and yoga under a trainer are ideal for them.
[23:45.20]A safe and comfortable living environment is important. 
[23:49.37]Providing safety to the elderly means preventing falls, protecting them from cheats, and being prepared for emergencies. 
[23:57.92]In addition, providing access to comfortable and easy transportation is needed. 
[24:04.20]The easy access helps them remain confident and independent and avoid feeling helpless.
[24:11.42]Mental condition should also be considered. 
[24:14.40]Providing social engagements for the elderly is extremely necessary for their mental wellness.
[24:20.70]Organising planned activities, events, or get-togethers for them with people of their age and their loved ones can encourage them to participate and stay involved. 
[24:32.21]Involving them in community activities or hobby clubs can also be helpful to give them a social circle of their liking.
[24:41.46]Understanding medical and healthcare needs is essential, too. 
[24:45.98]Elderly people often take multiple medicines at different times of the day. 
[24:51.67]Adhering to the prescription may become difficult when the number of medicines increases.
[24:58.03] Medication management becomes necessary. 
[25:01.53]If no one is available to assist, one can also use pill boxes to make it easy for the elderly. 
[25:09.32]However, supervision is required to ensure correct medicine administration. 
[25:15.50]Also, regular health checkups and vital checks are necessary for the elderly, depending on their health condition.
[25:24.03]Finally, offer financial and legal assistance. 
[25:28.33]Elderly people have concerns related to finance. 
[25:32.23]They require assistance with their pensions, savings, healthcare, taxes, etc. 
[25:38.78]It's important for them to receive safe and professional assistance with financial and legal processes from trusted sources.
[25:49.36]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.
